WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.840
Film Courage: Let’s take CLOSE ENCOUNTERS
OF THE THIRD KIND and [first] STAR WARS.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.410
Let’s say they both came out around the
same time.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:07.410
Houston Howard: They did [1977].

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.130
Film Courage: Why did one have a longer shelf
life and the other one is just this amazing,

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:15.900
nostalgic film that those of us of that time
remember fondly?

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.540
Houston Howard: It’s fascinating to look
at that case study because yeah, you have

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.730
two films that had similar budgets, both had
similar type of filmmakers.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.810
They were targeting a very similar audience,
they’re released at the same time, like

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.290
everything is the same.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:34.960
One becomes a really good movie that people
love and actually did pretty well (CLOSE ENCOUNTERS

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.149
OF THE THIRD KIND).

00:00:36.149 --> 00:00:39.760
Another becomes a 50 billion dollar brand.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:48.079
So there’s a variable there somewhere that
created that separation in the marketplace.

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:52.989
And we can look at that and probably guess
pretty closely why that happened.

00:00:52.989 --> 00:00:57.719
There’s a book that I read recently called
How Star Wars Conquered The Universe where

00:00:57.719 --> 00:01:02.409
they asked Steven Spielberg this exact same
question.

00:01:02.409 --> 00:01:06.100
Because him and [George] Lucas would actually
give each other producer points on all of

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.500
their movies.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.740
And so Lucas had producer points on Close
Encounters, Spielberg had producer points

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:14.780
on Star Wars.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.340
Obviously Spielberg got a better part of that
deal.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.120
And they said “Why did it work out so well
for Lucas?

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.400
And pretty well for you but not even in the
same ball park?”

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.700
He said there’s two reasons.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:33.299
He said from the beginning Lucas approached
this project not as just a single film, he

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:35.729
always had a broader vision.

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:37.649
He understood that there was story before
this.

00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:39.659
There is going to be story after this.

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:43.090
Maybe he didn’t have all the things planned
out exactly and have it all worked out.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:46.259
But his vision was very big from the beginning.

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:51.070
And secondly he had a world that people wanted
to explore, he had a story world.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:56.469
He said “I never even gave any thought of
my story world.

00:01:56.469 --> 00:02:00.390
The setting of Close Encounters is just our
world that is being invaded by aliens.”

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:06.439
But what he did, he created a world that people
wanted to continue to explore.

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:10.860
And if you can continue to create a world
in an IP (intellectual property) that people

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:17.050
want to continue to explore, then all of the
sudden that makes the room for the other things

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.570
that you want to do.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:22.500
And so what’s really interested about Lucas
in the same book is that, he didn’t grow

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:29.100
up wanting to be a filmmaker, he grew up wanting
to be an architect and he worked at his dad’s

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:32.020
toy store every summer as a kid.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:38.590
So he approached filmmaking not from the filmmaking
standpoint, he approached it from the architectural

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:43.000
and toy standpoint.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.510
He still went into make a good movie (of course).

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:51.900
But he then approached it, the industry and
the creative and the writing of it, in a completely

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.110
different way that then fueled all of these
opportunities.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:03:01.900
If you look at from the original Star Wars
movies, the cantina scene where Luke [Skywalker]

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.570
and Obi-Wan [Kenobi], they go into the cantina
and there’s all these crazy aliens around

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:10.310
and this is where they find Hans Solo for
the first time.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:20.050
There was a very specific choice of I want
the crazy aliens all around.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:23.910
To cut back on budget, they could have had
everybody look human like Luke if they wanted

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:32.580
to, but instead he really made sure that every
single character in that bar and in that cantina

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.600
had a very distinctive look, that they seem
mysterious, that it was obvious that all of

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.340
them had some sort of backstory involved and
just that created 1) The writing decision

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:47.790
and 2) The directorial decision (if you watch
the scene) the way they made sure they got

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:51.900
shots of all of them, there were interesting
ones in the foreground, in the background,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:59.260
they took time in the edit of the film to
cut…cut…cut…and show a handful of them.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:03.650
Then those creative opportunities, both on
the writing and directing and the editing

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:10.170
then fueled short stories and video games
and cartoons that dealt with all of them.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:14.440
They created web content for all of them,
they created game content for all of them

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.380
and they created toys for all of them.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:23.290
So now (all of the sudden) where most filmmakers
and directors would say “My scene is about

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:30.450
my protagonist and my antagonist and our central
characters, everything else is background.”

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.590
Lucas never considered anybody background.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:36.470
He looked at all of these other characters
as potential stories, potential stories, potential

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:39.730
stories that just so happened to be in the
background of this scene.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:45.520
But I’m going to develop all of them as
if they could be the heroes of their own story

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:46.920
and then put them in the background.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.740
So now the creative mindset is different when
he approaches the story.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:54.090
How do we do that as independent filmmakers
from my $100,000 feature?

00:04:54.090 --> 00:05:00.320
Well all of the sudden, the roommate of your
protagonist, now that’s just in the background

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.020
on a few different scenes.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:06.280
Now you should take the time to think How
do I make that guy interesting or that girl

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:07.280
interesting?

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.350
How do I give them an interesting look or
an interesting backstory?

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:15.110
How could I make them the protagonists of
their own story?

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:20.030
I’m going to develop them as if they could
be and then I’ll go back and put them in

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:21.030
the background.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:27.350
Now when I approach my short film, this really
interesting, weird, eclectic character now

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:33.440
in there that can spur a whole companion novel,
maybe it’s an EP of music, maybe it’s

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.630
a sole social media account for this crazy
character, it Tweeting back and forth leading

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:39.160
up or after the film.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:43.880
There’s lots of different things using the
principles that you see from Lucas and now

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.660
applying that into your specific smaller project.

